  overview="Jill is Philip Larkin's first novel, published by the Fortune Press in 1946. It follows John Kemp, a shy scholarship boy from a northern industrial town, through his first term at wartime Oxford, where he invents an imaginary girl to hold his own among wealthier undergraduates.",
  content="Kemp's arrival at Oxford and his uneasy dependence on his careless, public-school roommate Christopher Warner; his invention of 'Jill', a younger sister described in letters, a diary and a story; his infatuation with Gillian, a real schoolgirl who seems to be Jill made flesh; news that his home town has been bombed; and a party at which his hopes collapse.",
  summary="Lonely and out of place, Kemp tries to win Warner's approval by lending him money and running errands, and is mocked for his provincial background. To give himself a secret life he invents Jill, an idealised younger sister, writing her letters and a long story about her school. When he sees Gillian, a fifteen-year-old cousin of Elizabeth Dowling, a friend of Warner's, he believes he has found Jill and pursues her clumsily. An air raid on his home town sends him home, where he finds his parents safe but his former self strange to him. Back in Oxford, drunk at a party, he kisses Gillian, is roughly handled by Warner's friends and falls ill; the novel ends with his fantasy destroyed and his understanding of himself only beginning.",
  themes_text="The novel sets the consolations of fantasy against the humiliations of reality, a pattern that recurs throughout Larkin's poetry; it is also one of the first English novels about a working-class scholarship student at Oxford, alert to the snobbery and class anxiety of the place.",
  structure="A linear third-person narrative of one term, with Kemp's invented letters, diary and story embedded in the text.",
